From: Niluthon <37350260+Niluthon@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 11:43:17 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 04/13] B70-0043: hot/cold expert swapping
 (B70_MOE_HOTCOLD_SWAP): the VRAM hot set follows the traffic

Every B70_MOE_HOTCOLD_SWAP_S seconds (5) each rank scores its local experts per layer from the prefill route
counts of the last period (decay B70_MOE_HOTCOLD_SWAP_DECAY 0.95, plus 0.05 x the rankings-file order) and
swaps up to B70_MOE_HOTCOLD_SWAP_BUDGET (4) experts per layer whose cold score beats a hot one by 10 %: weights
and scales are exchanged between the hot slot and the cold pinned slot, and the expert maps are rewritten in
place, so the decode graphs need no recapture. It runs after the runner's per-step sync, waits for the 0042
prefetch stream, drops its staged layers, and synchronizes after the copies (7-16 ms per round).

The 0041 counter now keeps two tallies, eager batches (prefill) and graphs (decode); B70_MOE_ROUTE_COUNT=prefill
(implied by the swap switch) adds nothing to the decode graphs, and eager counting starts with the first real
batch so the memory-profiling run (and the KV size) is unchanged. The log line reports the last period only.

Measured on 2x B70 (136 hot, agentic coding sessions at temperature 0): 57-59 -> 66-76 tok/s; decode cold
share 30 % -> 18-21 %; KV unchanged (173,610 tokens).

Port to 0.32 (from Niluthon/qwen38-flash-tp2@e536e33, Apache-2.0, via our 0.30 carry): maybe_swap() is called next to maybe_dump_routes() at the top of Qwen4ExpModelState.prepare_inputs (its first .cpu() is the sync the 0.30 placement relied on).
